WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.360
I remember once working with a woman

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.520
who found it really frustrating
that someone in her office

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.480
loudly ate rice around
lunchtime every day

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.760
and her metal fork would constantly
be hitting against the bowl

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.280
and she was so annoyed by it
that she was actually going to go out

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.960
and buy a wooden fork
for this person.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.880
When you think we spend
half our waking life at work

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.920
if there's someone who
really gets under your skin,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.760
it's crucial to cross that divide.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.560
We've probably all worked
with people we don't like

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:50.960
but really?

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.440
You hate them,
you actually hate them?

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.600
That takes an enormous
amount of energy

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.040
and it makes little
or no difference to them.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.400
If they've really done something
totally illegal or horrendous

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.760
there's probably
much better remedies for it.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.800
Most of the time we find

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.880
that people want to avoid having
a conversation with the other person

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.040
because they dislike them,
but if you don’t talk about it

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.400
it won't get better by itself.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.040
So some people try to put it off
and develop coping mechanisms

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.160
but it rarely makes
the situation much better

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.680
and soon it's years down the line
and you're still in the same boat,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.040
hating this person.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.480
It doesn't have to be like this.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.440
A bit of short term pain,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.440
i.e. a difficult but open
and honest conversation

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.760
with the person you dislike,
can bring a long-term gain.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.080
We get people telling us
we're intimidating

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.920
when we think we're really cuddly

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.480
or telling us that we're shy

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.280
when we consider ourselves
thoughtful and serious.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.480
So think about what you
give off to other people.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.760
Here's Ali. He's very bright.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.240
He's quite young,
he's very highly educated.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.920
He's just got a great job
so he's sent to a conference

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.240
and because, as well as being bright,
he's rather modest

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.640
he decides that he'll keep quiet,
pretty much,

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.760
and he'll just observe
what's going on

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.840
and listen to people
who are more experienced.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.720
The people around him

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.320
who have heard how bright he is
and how well-educated

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.760
see him being a bit restrained
and a bit withheld

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.280
and start to think,
"He's a bit aloof."

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.560
"He doesn't rate us."

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.280
"He thinks he's too good for us."
And so they start to freeze him out.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.240
We end up with a situation where

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.000
everybody's misunderstanding
each other and nobody

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.360
is getting the benefit of their
different abilities and experiences.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.200
Ask questions and seek to understand
the other person's viewpoint.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.800
Then show them
you've understood their viewpoint

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.960
by summarising back to them
what they have said -

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.840
an incredibly powerful little tool
for building rapport.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.040
Too often, people just try to
persuade others of their case

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.160
but if you show that you're open
to listening to the other person

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.320
and genuinely want to understand
where they're coming from,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.240
you'll have a much more
constructive conversation.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.520
I remember once in a mediation
where one person shouted at the other

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.920
"You're a..."

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.800
Now I don’t have a problem
with someone saying that

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:15.880
if that's how they feel

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.120
but I do actually
with how it's worded.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.560
If you tell someone they're a liar,
they'll automatically disagree.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.440
We all would.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.080
But if you calmly point out

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.880
that on this occasion and
this occasion they have lied to you

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.880
you can have a conversation about it
without them getting as defensive.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.200
It's probably not personal.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:37.840
Remember that you don't have to

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.800
learn to like the person
that you're in conflict with -

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.000
you just have to work with them.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.480
Be willing to challenge your
assumptions and then learn from that.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.440
Welcome other's ideas and approaches.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.680
Be yourself, be open, be honest about
your own strengths and contributions.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.080
The really great thing is
if you're doing those things,

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.120
not only does it diminish
that feeling of dislike

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.680
and make you better
at working with people,

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.520
but you might actually get
to enjoy working with them.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.500
If you liked that, subscribe to the BBC Ideas YouTube channel.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.000
Lots more top tips on how to be successful at work.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

